Breakdown.CellLayerSystem.breakdownOnSpa.text:
Assume one collection of finite profile, pressure, mesh, cell-matrix, tree, multiplier, adjoint and offset data satisfies all fifteen groups of the cell conditions, including their functional, integral and numerical inequalities. The formal statement concludes two assertions. For every viscosity $
u>0$, there exists a smooth compactly supported divergence-free datum on $\mathbb R^3$ for which no smooth solution on all $t\ge0$ has a uniformly bounded $L^2$ norm. Also, for every $
u>0$, there exists a smooth divergence-free datum on $\mathbb R^3$ that is periodic under each unit coordinate translation and has no smooth solution on all $t\ge0$ with periodic velocity. The latter represents the unit torus. Velocity and pressure are functions on all real times and points, with smoothness required on $[0,\infty)	imes\mathbb R^3$, the initial condition at zero, and the equations for $t>0$. The torus conclusion does not require pressure periodicity. The formal conclusions are nonexistence statements for global smooth solutions, with the stated whole-space energy restriction; this theorem does not itself display a blow-up time or a divergent norm. It remains conditional on a data set satisfying the cell conditions, whose existence is not an output of this theorem.

The complete grouping of the conditions assumed here is as follows. The hypothesis is a condition on one consistent choice of seven collections of data: the certificate fields and mesh; their concrete bounds; the cell forms and their expression in common coordinates; the matrix tree; the exterior, omitted-mode and error data; four coercive-estimate numbers; and the profile, pressure, adjoint and offset representations. It requires fifteen groups of assertions. These are: (1) the geometry, symmetries, row fields, reference field and matrix-tree hypotheses; (2) the identities and value bounds of the represented fields, including positive periods and constant weight $w_*=39/50$; (3) the conditions assembling the cell, exterior and error contributions; (4) admissibility of the multiplier bounds for every $\alpha\in\mathcal K$; (5) $c_1\ge\sqrt{50/3}\,c_{\mathsf M}$, where $c_{\mathsf M}^2=(50/3)(1+\mathsf n_\lambda+\mathsf n_{
abla\lambda}/\sqrt{w_*})^2+\mathsf n_\mu/c_\mu$; (6) $c_2\ge\sqrt{1000\cdot50/3}$; (7) the remaining sign, norm, invertibility, smallness and parameter-inclusion inequalities of the certificate; (8) valid profile, pressure and adjoint representations with their identifications to the same certificate; (9) the affine inverse-period and forcing identities, offset coefficient enclosures and exterior bounds; (10) the four profile-change norms and their two scalar budgets; (11) the reference-strain and $L^3$ gradient bounds and their budget; (12) the $L^2$ or $L^3$ adjoint norms and quadratic budgets; (13) identification of central and directional profiles and a fixed adjoint, with no separately fitted directions; (14) valid finite potential and compact constraint-generator representations, identified with those in the adjoint estimate; and (15) the central residual, directional, time and constraint replacement bounds whose total is at most each $a_j$. In (5), $\mathsf n_\lambda$ and $\mathsf n_{
abla\lambda}$ bound the multiplier error and its gradient, $\mathsf n_\mu$ is the velocity-piece ratio bound, and $c_\mu>0$ is its multiplier constant. The cell coercivity and constraint weight are fixed to $3/50$ and $1000$. The row bound also requires $c_0^2\ge(50/3)400(1+2^{-18})$. Fields live on $\mathbb R_	heta	imes\mathbb R_y^3$ and all period integrals use $[0,1]$, equivalent to $(0,1)$. These conditions include functional identities, pointwise inequalities, weak derivatives, integrals and matrix inequalities. Finitely represented inputs do not make these assertions automatic, and this theorem gives no concrete data set satisfying them.
